1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,329
An AI can finish a task and still deliver the
wrong thing. What matters is whether the

2
00:00:06,329 --> 00:00:08,474
result matches what you agreed to.

3
00:00:09,324 --> 00:00:14,856
Meet PromiseKeeper. One Switch room brings
you, a coordinator, and a builder together.

4
00:00:14,856 --> 00:00:20,389
You confirm the request. The agents do the
work and check the result.

5
00:00:21,239 --> 00:00:27,615
Here’s the request: export three sample
orders with order number and total only. The

6
00:00:27,615 --> 00:00:31,258
coordinator confirms the details before the
builder starts.

7
00:00:32,109 --> 00:00:38,264
For this demo, we start with a deliberately
flawed export. It includes email and

8
00:00:38,264 --> 00:00:43,540
internal notes. The program runs, but the
check catches those extra columns.

9
00:00:44,390 --> 00:00:49,733
The coordinator sends the failure back
through Switch. The builder fixes the
exporter

10
00:00:49,733 --> 00:00:53,843
and generates a new file. The same checks now
pass.

11
00:00:54,693 --> 00:01:00,684
You inspect the file and approve that checked
version. Its receipt connects your review

12
00:01:00,684 --> 00:01:06,247
to the exact file. In this prototype, review
happens through a local command.

13
00:01:07,097 --> 00:01:13,387
Now add email to the request. PromiseKeeper
records a new version and clears the old

14
00:01:13,387 --> 00:01:17,580
approval. The changed request needs a fresh
check and review.

15
00:01:18,430 --> 00:01:24,282
That’s PromiseKeeper: a shared task with a
verifiable result. Real agent handoffs

16
00:01:24,282 --> 00:01:31,108
through Switch, a human review step, and nine
passing tests. A changed request never

17
00:01:31,108 --> 00:01:33,059
inherits an old approval.
